  "seoBody": "<div class=\"key-takeaways\"><strong>Key takeaways</strong><ul><li>An EV charge point installation is notifiable electrical work under BS 7671 and Part P, with a dedicated section covering EV charging equipment</li><li>PEN fault protection is the headline safety issue: open-PEN detection or an alternative earthing arrangement is required on most TN-C-S supplies</li><li>The record captures the earthing arrangement, RCD type, PEN fault protection method, load management, and the commissioning test results</li><li>OZEV grant routes and the socket and safety standards set what the install and paperwork must show</li><li>Software captures the data and generates the report; it does not replace a competent installer or a calibrated multifunction tester</li></ul></div><h2>What EV charge point install software is actually for</h2><p>An electric vehicle charge point installation is an electrical installation, governed by BS 7671, the 18th Edition wiring regulations, and notifiable under Part P in England and Wales. BS 7671 has a specific section for EV charging equipment, and the IET publishes a Code of Practice for Electric Vehicle Charging Equipment Installation. Grant-funded work under an OZEV scheme adds its own evidence and standards requirements.</p><p>Software does not make any of that valid. The competent installer does, working to the standards with calibrated instruments. What software does is make the installation and commissioning record easier to complete correctly on site, harder to forget the PEN fault check or the RCD type, and faster to deliver as a clean report. A good tool means the installer does not reconstruct the commissioning data from memory in the van on the way home.</p><h2>PEN fault protection and earthing</h2><p>The defining safety issue for domestic EV charging is the protective earth. On a TN-C-S (PME) supply, an open-PEN fault can put a dangerous voltage on the vehicle and its casing. BS 7671 requires the installation to guard against this, typically with a charge point that has open-PEN detection built in, or an alternative earthing arrangement such as an earth electrode meeting the required conditions. The record has to show which method was used and why. Quickler prompts the installer to state the supply earthing arrangement, the PEN fault protection method and the RCD type, and to photograph the consumer unit, the charge point and any earth electrode. It does not decide the earthing design; the competent installer does, to the current edition of BS 7671.</p><h2>Load management, RCD type and the commissioning tests</h2><h3>1. RCD type and protective devices</h3><p>The RCD type appropriate to the equipment, and the cable and protective device selection, recorded and photographed.</p><h3>2. Load management</h3><p>Any load management or CT clamp arrangement that limits demand on the supply, with its settings recorded.</p><h3>3. Commissioning tests</h3><p>Continuity, insulation resistance, earth fault loop impedance, RCD operation and polarity, read off a calibrated multifunction tester.</p><h3>4. Function and labelling</h3><p>Confirm the unit charges and the labelling and signage are in place, with photos at the point of test.</p><h2>The certificate and the customer record</h2><p>The customer should leave with the electrical installation certificate or minor works certificate for the charge point, the commissioning results, the equipment and warranty details, and clear guidance on the charger. Where the work was grant funded, the evidence pack has to satisfy that scheme's requirements too. Quickler pulls the installation data and photos into one report the moment the job is signed off. It complements the electrical certificate; it does not replace it. Check the current OZEV scheme rules and BS 7671 requirements before relying on any template. This is guidance, not legal advice.</p><h2>What to look for in installation report software</h2><ul><li><strong>On-site completion:</strong> the tool should make it easier to record the install during the job, not afterwards</li><li><strong>Photo evidence at the point of test:</strong> consumer unit, charge point and earth electrode tied to a timestamp, not a folder sorted out that evening</li><li><strong>Clean report output:</strong> a customer record and grant evidence pack accepted without explanation</li><li><strong>No per-seat penalty:</strong> per-seat pricing charges for office staff and subcontractors who never file. Quickler is £20 per active user per month, dormant users free, everything unlimited</li><li><strong>Evidence trail:</strong> timestamp, installer, address and every reading, for the insurer or dispute that surfaces years later</li></ul><div class=\"callout\"><strong>Honest note:</strong> Quickler captures the installation data and generates the report. It does not make a charge point safe, it does not design the earthing, and it is not a measuring instrument. It records the values the installer reads off a calibrated tester. The competent installer and the calibrated instrument carry the validity. <a href=\"/pages/help/index.html#contact-form-section\">Ask us</a> if that distinction matters for your firm.</div><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>What is an EV charge point installation report?</h3><p>It is the record made when an electric vehicle charge point is installed and commissioned: the supply earthing arrangement, the PEN fault protection method, the RCD type, any load management, and the commissioning tests such as loop impedance and RCD operation, with photos of the consumer unit and charge point. It sits alongside the electrical installation certificate for the job.</p><h3>What is PEN fault protection and why does it matter for EV chargers?</h3><p>On a TN-C-S (PME) supply, an open-PEN fault can make the vehicle and charge point casing live at a dangerous voltage. BS 7671 requires the installation to protect against this, usually with a charge point that has open-PEN detection or an alternative earthing arrangement such as a compliant earth electrode. The installation record should state which method was used. Confirm the requirements against the current edition of BS 7671.</p><h3>Does an EV charger install need to be notified?</h3><p>Yes. Installing a charge point is notifiable electrical work under Part P in England and Wales, and it must comply with BS 7671, which includes a specific section for EV charging equipment. A registered competent person can self-certify. Check the current Part P and BS 7671 requirements, and any OZEV grant scheme rules if the work is grant funded.</p><h3>Can I record an EV charger install over WhatsApp?</h3><p>Yes. Quickler's installation workflow runs over the WhatsApp Business API. The installer receives each question in their existing WhatsApp chat, replies with the reading, a voice note or a photo, and the report generates automatically. No separate app or login is required, and Quickler manages the WhatsApp Business API account on the firm's behalf. It records the values the installer reads off a calibrated tester; it is not the tester.</p><h3>Related guides</h3><ul><li><a href=\"/pages/solar-pv-commissioning-report-uk.html\">Solar PV commissioning report</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/heat-pump-commissioning-checklist-uk.html\">Heat pump commissioning checklist</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/battery-storage-inspection-report-uk.html\">Battery storage inspection report</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/eicr-software-uk.html\">EICR and electrical reporting software</a></li></ul>",
